# Experiment 5: Dynamic React Form

### 1. Aim
To create a React form whose input fields are generated dynamically using a JavaScript configuration object.

### 2. Create the project

```bash
npm create vite@latest dynamic-form -- --template react
cd dynamic-form
npm install
npm run dev
```

### 3. Write the code

Replace `src/App.jsx` with:

```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

const fields = [
  { name: "name", label: "Name", type: "text" },
  { name: "email", label: "Email", type: "email" },
  { name: "mobile", label: "Mobile Number", type: "tel" },
  { name: "age", label: "Age", type: "number" },
  { name: "city", label: "City", type: "text" }
];

export default function App() {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});
  const [submitted, setSubmitted] = useState(null);

  function handleChange(e) {
    const { name, value } = e.target;

    setFormData({
      ...formData,
      [name]: value
    });
  }

  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    setSubmitted({ ...formData });
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Dynamic Registration Form</h2>

      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
        {fields.map((field) => (
          <div key={field.name}>
            <label>{field.label}: </label>

            <input
              name={field.name}
              type={field.type}
              value={formData[field.name] || ""}
              onChange={handleChange}
            />

            <br /><br />
          </div>
        ))}

        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
      </form>

      {submitted && (
        <div>
          <h3>Submitted Details</h3>

          {fields.map((field) => (
            <p key={field.name}>
              {field.label}: {submitted[field.name] || ""}
            </p>
          ))}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 4. Expected output

The page displays:

```text
Dynamic Registration Form

Name:          [____________]
Email:         [____________]
Mobile Number: [____________]
Age:           [____________]
City:          [____________]

[Submit]
```

After entering the details and clicking Submit:

```text
Submitted Details

Name: John Smith
Email: john@example.com
Mobile Number: 9876543210
Age: 22
City: Hyderabad
```

### 5. Understand these three concepts

**A. Configuration object**

```jsx
const fields = [
  { name: "name", label: "Name", type: "text" },
  { name: "email", label: "Email", type: "email" }
];
```

It stores the details of the input fields.

To add another field, add another object to the array.

**B. Dynamic field generation**

```jsx
{fields.map((field) => (
  <input
    key={field.name}
    name={field.name}
    type={field.type}
    onChange={handleChange}
  />
))}
```

The `map()` method generates an input for every object in the array.

**C. Updating form state**

```jsx
setFormData({
  ...formData,
  [name]: value
});
```

- `...formData` preserves existing values.
- `[name]: value` updates the field that changed.

### 6. Important viva questions

**Q1. What is a dynamic form?**

A form whose input fields are generated programmatically from a configuration object.

**Q2. Why do we use `map()`?**

To iterate over the configuration array and generate input fields dynamically.

**Q3. What is `useState()`?**

A React Hook that allows functional components to store and update state.

**Q4. What is `onChange`?**

An event handler that runs when the input value changes.

**Q5. Why do we use `e.preventDefault()`?**

To prevent the browser's default form submission, which would otherwise normally reload the page.

**Q6. What does the spread operator (`...`) do?**

It expands the properties of an existing object, allowing us to preserve previous form values while updating one field.

