# Experiment 6: React SPA with Routing and API Fetching

### 1. Aim
To develop a Single Page Application with Home and Users pages, fetch user data from a public API, and display a loading indicator while the data is being fetched.

### 2. Create the project

```bash
npm create vite@latest react-spa -- --template react
cd react-spa
npm install
npm install react-router-dom
npm run dev
```

**Note:** This experiment requires an internet connection to retrieve the user data.

### 3. Write the code

Replace `src/App.jsx` with:

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route, Link } from "react-router-dom";

function Home() {
  return <h2>Home Page</h2>;
}

function Users() {
  const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users")
      .then((response) => {
        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error("Failed to fetch users");
        }
        return response.json();
      })
      .then((data) => {
        setUsers(data);
        setLoading(false);
      })
      .catch(() => {
        setLoading(false);
      });
  }, []);

  if (loading) {
    return <h3>Loading...</h3>;
  }

  if (users.length === 0) {
    return <h3>Unable to load users.</h3>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Users List</h2>

      {users.map((user) => (
        <p key={user.id}>{user.name}</p>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

export default function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      <nav>
        <Link to="/">Home</Link> |{" "}
        <Link to="/users">Users</Link>
      </nav>

      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/users" element={<Users />} />
      </Routes>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}
```

### 4. Expected output

When you open the application:

```text
Home | Users

Home Page
```

Click Users:

```text
Home | Users

Users List

Leanne Graham
Ervin Howell
Clementine Bauch
Patricia Lebsack
Chelsey Dietrich
...
```

While the request is in progress, the application displays:

```text
Loading...
```

These user names and the API URL match the example in your lab manual. SDC 3 Practical Manual With Cer…

### 5. Understand these important lines

**A. `useState()`**

```jsx
const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
```

- `users` stores the fetched user data.
- `loading` tracks whether the request is in progress.

**B. `useEffect()`**

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users")
    .then((response) => response.json())
    .then((data) => {
      setUsers(data);
      setLoading(false);
    });
}, []);
```

It runs the API-fetching operation after the component mounts. The empty dependency array means the effect is intended to run once per mount.

**C. `fetch()`**

It sends an HTTP request to retrieve data from the API.

**D. `response.json()`**

It converts the response body into JavaScript data.

**E. `users.map()`**

It generates a paragraph for every user returned by the API.

### 6. Important viva questions

**Q1. What is an SPA?**

A Single Page Application updates its displayed content dynamically without reloading the entire page during normal navigation.

**Q2. What is an API?**

An Application Programming Interface allows different software applications to communicate with one another.

**Q3. What is `useEffect()`?**

A React Hook used to perform side effects, such as fetching data from an API.

**Q4. Why is the dependency array empty?**

To make the effect run once per component mount, rather than after every render.

**Q5. What is JSON?**

JavaScript Object Notation is a text-based data format commonly used to exchange data between clients and servers.

**Q6. Why do we show Loading?**

Because the API request takes time, and the loading indicator informs the user that data is being retrieved.

**Q7. What happens if the API fails?**

The program above displays `Unable to load users.` if the request fails or returns an empty list.

